.TH PKCSICSF 1 "August 2026" "@PACKAGE_VERSION@" "openCryptoki"
.SH NAME
pkcsicsf \- configuration utility for the ICSF token

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpkcsicsf\fP
[\fB-h\fP]
[\fB-l\fP]
[\fB-a\fP \fItoken-name\fP]
\fB-m\fP \fImechanism\fP
[\fB-u\fP \fIURI\fP]
[\fB-b\fP \fIBINDDN\fP]
[\fB-c\fP \fIclient-cert-file\fP]
[\fB-C\fP \fICA-cert-file\fP]
[\fB-k\fP \fIprivatekey\fP]

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBpkcsicsf\fP utility lists available ICSF tokens and allows user
to add one specific ICSF token to opencryptoki.

The ICSF token must be added first to opencryptoki. This creates an
entry in the opencryptoki.conf file for the ICSF token. It also creates
a \fItoken_name.conf\fP configuration file in the same directory as
the opencryptoki.conf file, containing ICSF specific information.
This information is read by the ICSF token.

The ICSF token must bind and authenticate to an LDAP server.
The supported authentication mechanisms are simple and sasl.
One of these mechanisms must be entered when listing the available
ICSF tokens or when adding an ICSF token.

The system admin can either allow the ldap calls to utilize existing
ldap configs, such as ldap.conf or .ldaprc for bind and
authentication information or set the bind and authentication
information within opencryptoki by using this utility and its options.
The information will then be placed in the \fItoken_name.conf\fP file
to be used in the ldap calls. When using simple authentication,
the user will be prompted for the racf password when listing
or adding a token.

The \fBpkcsicsf\fP utility must be run as root when adding an ICSF token to
opencryptoki.

.SH "TOKEN INITIALIZATION"
Unlike other openCryptoki tokens, the ICSF token does \fBnot\fP require
\fBC_InitToken\fP (i.e.
.BR pkcsconf (1)
\fB-I\fP) to be run after adding the token.
The cryptographic objects and key material reside on the remote z/OS ICSF
server, which is already fully provisioned before \fBpkcsicsf\fP is invoked.
Running \fBC_InitToken\fP on an ICSF token is therefore unnecessary.

When \fBpkcsicsf \-a\fP succeeds it performs the full local initialization
automatically:
.RS
.IP \(bu 4
Creates the token directory under \fI@localstatedir@/lib/opencryptoki/\fP.
.IP \(bu 4
Creates the ICSF-specific configuration file
\fI@sysconfdir@/opencryptoki/token_name.conf\fP.
.IP \(bu 4
Writes the initial token data file (\fINVTOK.DAT\fP) with:
.RS
.IP \(bu 4
The SO PIN supplied during \fBpkcsicsf \-a\fP.
.IP \(bu 4
\fBCKF_TOKEN_INITIALIZED\fP already set \(em no further init step is needed.
.IP \(bu 4
\fBCKF_USER_PIN_TO_BE_CHANGED\fP set, indicating the user PIN must be
established before first use.
.RE
.IP \(bu 4
Writes the RACF file (\fIRACF\fP) with the encrypted RACF password.
.RE

The correct post-installation sequence is therefore:
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fP
Add the token (run as root):
.PP
.RS
\fBpkcsicsf \-a \fItoken-name\fB \-m simple \-u ldap://\fIhost\fR\fP
.RE
.TP
\fB2.\fP
Restart the slot daemon so the new token is visible:
.PP
.RS
\fBsystemctl restart pkcsslotd\fP
.RE
.TP
\fB3.\fP
Initialize the user PIN (run as SO):
.PP
.RS
\fBpkcsconf \-u \-c \fIslot\fR\fP
.RE
.RE
.PP
After step\~3 the token is fully operational and
\fBCKF_TOKEN_INITIALIZED\fP will be reported by
.BR pkcsconf (1)
\fB\-t\fP.

.SH "OPTIONS"
.IP "\fB-a\fP \fItoken name\fP" 10
Add the specified ICSF token to opencryptoki.
See
.B TOKEN INITIALIZATION
above for the steps that follow. To add all available ICSF token,
specify \fBall\fP.
.IP "\fB-b\fP \fIBINDDN\fP" 10
The distinguished name to bind when using simple authentication.
.IP "\fB-c\fP \fIclient-cert-file\fP" 10
The client certificate file for SASL authentication.
If not specified, the value from \fBldap.conf\fP(5) or \fI~/.ldaprc\fP is used.
.IP "\fB-C\fP \fICA-cert-file\fP" 10
The CA certificate file for SASL authentication.
If not specified, the value from \fBldap.conf\fP(5) or \fI~/.ldaprc\fP is used.
.IP "\fB-h\fP" 10
Show usage information.
.IP "\fB-k\fP \fIprivatekey\fP" 10
The client private key file for SASL authentication.
If not specified, the value from \fBldap.conf\fP(5) or \fI~/.ldaprc\fP is used.
.IP "\fB-l\fP" 10
List available ICSF tokens.
.IP "\fB-m\fP \fImechanism\fP" 10
The authentication mechanism to use when binding to the LDAP server
(either \fBsimple\fP or \fBsasl\fP).
This option is required whenever \fB\-l\fP or \fB\-a\fP is used.
.IP "\fB-u\fP \fIURI\fP" 10
The URI of the LDAP server (e.g. \fBldap://hostname\fP or
\fBldaps://hostname\fP).

.SH "FILES"
.IP "@sysconfdir@/opencryptoki/opencryptoki.conf"
The opencryptoki config file containing token configuration information.
.IP "@sysconfdir@/opencryptoki/\fItoken_name\fP.conf"
Contains ICSF configuration information for the ICSF token.
.IP "@localstatedir@/lib/opencryptoki/\fItoken_name\fP/NVTOK.DAT"
Local token data file: stores PIN hashes and token flags including
\fBCKF_TOKEN_INITIALIZED\fP.
Written by \fBpkcsicsf \-a\fP and updated by \fBpkcsconf\fP.
.IP "@localstatedir@/lib/opencryptoki/\fItoken_name\fP/RACF"
Contains the RACF password encrypted with the token's master key.

.SH SEE ALSO
.PD 0
.TP
\fBopencryptoki\fP(7),
.TP
\fBpkcsslotd\fP(8),
.TP
\fBpkcsconf\fP(1).
.PD
